# Git must not rewrite the bytes of anything in this repository.
#
# `dc-evidence` binds a bundle to its contract by digesting the contract's
# exact bytes — `lib.rs`: "SHA-256 of exact bytes; never hash independently
# reserialized JSON" — and `fixtures/v1/bundle*.json` record those digests as
# literals. `include_bytes!` then hands the test binary whatever the checkout
# wrote to disk. So a checkout that converts LF to CRLF changes the digest of
# `contract.json` and `contract-not-found.json` and nothing else visible: the
# JSON still parses, the semantics are unchanged, and eleven verdicts in
# `dc-evidence/tests/evidence.rs` flip to `Failed` while naming no cause.
#
# That is not hypothetical. Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`
# in its system config and `actions/checkout` does not override it, so with no
# `.gitattributes` here the `components (windows-latest)` job was red on every
# push from 2026-09-10 onward. It also took `cargo test` down with it before
# dc-glob, dc-proc, dc-grep, dc-store or dc-verify ever ran, which is most of
# what that job exists to answer.
#
# `-text` rather than `text=auto eol=lf`: some committed files are recorded
# evidence that genuinely contains CRLF — `benchmarks/results/**` captures
# competitor stdout verbatim — and normalising would silently rewrite the
# record. Nothing in this tree wants end-of-line translation, so nothing is
# exempted below. Keep it that way: a `text` entry added here re-arms the bug
# for whatever it covers.
* -text
